# The Genomic Mosaic of the Damask Rose

## A Haplotype-Resolved Framework for Distinguishing Ancient Hybridization, Introgression, and Incomplete Lineage Sorting

**Theoretical PhD Research Proposal — Fourth Draft v0.4**

**Ouadi Maakoul**
*For Humanity 4*

---

## Abstract

The evolutionary origin of the Damask rose, *Rosa × damascena*, remains a compelling problem in plant evolutionary genomics because its genome may preserve signals from multiple ancestral lineages while also being shaped by polyploidy, recombination, introgression, incomplete lineage sorting, clonal propagation, and human selection.

The 2026 *Rosa* pangenome provides an unprecedented genomic foundation, assembling 51 haplotypes from 23 accessions and identifying approximately 1.8 million structural variants. This resource makes it possible to move from marker-based ancestry inference toward haplotype-resolved, genome-wide reconstruction of reticulate evolutionary history.

The central research question is:

> **Can the present nuclear genome of *Rosa × damascena* be statistically decomposed into ancestry blocks in a manner that distinguishes ancient hybridization from subsequent introgression and incomplete lineage sorting?**

This fourth draft responds to critical feedback on its predecessor and implements seven decisive operational upgrades. **First**, it includes a concrete, one-page Phase 0 audit table with pre-specified thresholds and contingency plans, forcing an explicit go/no-go decision before any model specification. **Second**, it pre-registers a simplification rule for the mixture-model HMM: if phasing quality falls below defined thresholds, the inheritance-mode layer \(c_i\) will be fixed based on cytogenetic evidence, and only ancestry \(z_i\) will be inferred. **Third**, it specifies a simulation-based power analysis for DFOIL/Δ-statistics on tetraploid data, quantifying expected power loss under collapsed pseudo-diploid representation. **Fourth**, it pre-registers ABC-RF priors for all demographic parameters. **Fifth**, it operationalizes the recombination-rate confound control for the SV/LD test using ANCOVA with local repeat density, GC content, and background recombination rate as covariates. **Sixth**, it commits to a concrete time-aware HMM extension that models clonal propagation as a regime switch in the ancestry transition matrix. **Seventh**, it integrates *twisst2* and *sticcs* as phasing-free robustness controls, ensuring that conclusions do not depend on the success of polyploid phasing.

The deliverable remains a reproducible computational framework and a probabilistic, haplotype-resolved map of evolutionary history across the *R. × damascena* genome, with pre-registered quantitative falsification criteria.

