## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 The evolutionary problem

A present-day genome is not necessarily the product of a single uninterrupted lineage.

In sexually reproducing organisms, recombination partitions ancestral chromosomes into segments with potentially different evolutionary histories. Hybridization can introduce DNA from divergent lineages. Introgression can subsequently transfer genomic regions across species boundaries. Polyploidization preserves multiple homologous or homeologous chromosome sets. Incomplete lineage sorting produces gene trees that disagree with the species tree even without recent gene flow.

Consequently, at the level of individual genomic loci:

$$
\text{one organism} \neq \text{one evolutionary history}
$$

The Damask rose provides an exceptionally instructive case. *Rosa × damascena* is tetraploid (2n = 4x = 56) and is generally described as having a probable triparental origin involving *R. moschata*, *R. gallica*, and *R. fedtschenkoana*.

The 2026 *Rosa* pangenome substantially changes the methodological landscape. It assembled 51 haplotypes from 23 accessions and identified 1,801,537 structural variants, revealing extensive introgression across sections. The pangenome reports high assembly quality: average BUSCO completeness of 97.6% across all haplotypes, and LAI values averaging 21.06. However, the tetraploid *R. gallica* assembly was flagged during peer review as fragmented, with more than 3,514 contigs, suggesting problems during assembly and phasing.

This creates a precise scientific opportunity. The question is no longer whether the Damask rose is a hybrid—that is established. The question is:

> **What genomic architecture would each competing evolutionary history produce, and can haplotype-resolved genomic data distinguish those architectures?**

