## 3. Research Questions and Hypotheses

### 3.1 Central research question

> **Can haplotype-resolved nuclear genomic data distinguish the ancestral genomic contributions to *Rosa × damascena* from later introgression and incomplete lineage sorting?**

### 3.2 Secondary questions

**Q1. Ancestry distribution.** Do different genomic regions of *R. × damascena* show statistically distinguishable affinities to candidate ancestral lineages?

**Q2. Spatial coherence.** Are these affinities organized into contiguous genomic blocks rather than randomly distributed markers?

**Q3. Inheritance mode.** Does the tetraploid genome exhibit detectable variation in inheritance mode (disomic vs. polysomic) across chromosomes, and does this variation affect ancestry inference?

**Q4. Directionality.** If introgression occurred, what was its direction—which lineage donated and which received?

**Q5. Model comparison.** Which of the three competing evolutionary models best explains the observed genomic data under formal statistical comparison?

**Q6. Structural variation.** Do structural variants, particularly inversions, correspond to ancestry boundaries or ancestry-enriched regions, and do they suppress recombination within blocks after controlling for background recombination rate?

**Q7. Robustness.** Are inferred ancestry patterns stable under changes in reference genome, candidate donor set, genomic window size, marker filtering, and statistical model?

**Q8. Identifiability.** Can the proposed mixture-model HMM recover known ancestry, inheritance mode, and reference-bias parameters under realistic data quality conditions?

**Q9. Phasing-free control.** Do ancestry patterns inferred from unphased data using *twisst2*/*sticcs* converge with patterns inferred from phased data using the mixture HMM?

### 3.3 Hypotheses

The central hypothesis is:

$$
H_1: \text{The nuclear genome of }R.\times damascena \text{ contains a non-random, spatially structured ancestry mosaic.}
$$

The null hypothesis is subdivided into mechanistic alternatives:

$$
H_{0a} = \text{incomplete lineage sorting}
$$

$$
H_{0b} = \text{later introgression}
$$

$$
H_{0c} = \text{reference/sampling/methodological artifact}
$$

$$
H_{0d} = \text{ghost lineage introgression}
$$

This formulation prevents the analysis from becoming a search for evidence supporting a predetermined triparental hypothesis.

