## 7. The Ancestry-Block Model with Inheritance-Mode Mixture

### 7.1 Why a standard HMM is insufficient

A standard hidden Markov model assumes a single, uniform process generating ancestry transitions across all genomic windows. This assumption is violated in *R. × damascena* because the tetraploid genome may exhibit both disomic and polysomic inheritance in different regions.

### 7.2 Proposed mixture-model HMM

We propose a mixture-model HMM in which each genomic window is assigned a latent inheritance mode \(c_i \in \{\text{disomic}, \text{polysomic}\}\). The joint probability becomes:

$$
P(Z, C, D) = P(z_1) \prod_{i=2}^{m} P(z_i \mid z_{i-1}, c_i) \prod_{i=1}^{m} P(c_i \mid c_{i-1}) \prod_{i=1}^{m} P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i)
$$

where:

- \(C = \{c_1, \ldots, c_m\}\) is the sequence of inheritance modes;
- \(P(z_i \mid z_{i-1}, c_i)\) is the ancestry transition probability conditional on inheritance mode;
- \(P(c_i \mid c_{i-1})\) models the spatial persistence of inheritance mode;
- \(P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i)\) is the emission probability, which incorporates allele dosage expectations under each mode.

### 7.3 Identifiability check (critical prerequisite)

**Before committing to this architecture, run a simulation-based identifiability check.** Simulate genomes under known \((z, c, \beta)\) and confirm the model recovers them under realistic (not idealized) data quality per donor. Quantify the **confusion matrix**—how often is a polysomic locus misclassified as disomic?

**Pre-registered performance thresholds:**

| Parameter | Threshold | Action if violated |
|---|---|---|
| Ancestry assignment accuracy | > 80% at 10× coverage | Downgrade donor to "unresolved" |
| Inheritance-mode confusion | < 20% misclassification | Fix \(c_i\) from cytogenetics |
| \(\beta_r\) estimation correlation | > 0.7 | Use externally calibrated prior only |
| Block N50 (phased) | > 1 Mb median | Switch to pseudo-diploid |

If recovery is poor for weakly-sequenced donors, this is useful information for Phase 0, not a failure of the modeling. In that case, simplify the model by **fixing the inheritance mode** based on known cytogenetic data for *R. damascena* and only estimating ancestry probabilities.

### 7.4 Reference-bias modeling

The emission probability must account for reference bias. We model this as:

$$
P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i) = \sum_{r} P(D_i \mid z_i, c_i, \beta_r) P(\beta_r)
$$

where \(\beta_r\) is a reference-bias parameter for candidate lineage \(r\). **These parameters will be estimated externally** using simulation: simulate reads from a high-quality genome of lineage \(r\) at various divergence levels from the reference, map them to the reference, and measure the mapping bias. Use these externally calibrated priors in the HMM rather than estimating bias from the same data used for ancestry inference.

**Pre-registered divergence levels for \(\beta_r(d)\) estimation:** 0%, 1%, 3%, 5% divergence from reference. The resulting function \(\beta_r(d)\) will be used as an informative prior, not estimated jointly.

