## 9. Statistical Discrimination

### 9.1 Formal model comparison via ABC-random forest

For each evolutionary model \(M_j\), we estimate:

$$
P(D \mid M_j)
$$

using **Approximate Bayesian Computation with random forest** (ABC-RF). Classical rejection- or regression-ABC scales poorly with the high-dimensional summary vector required here (block-length moments, Twisst weights, DFOIL/Δ, \(F_{ST}\), SV enrichment, LD decay). ABC-RF tolerates high-dimensional, possibly non-sufficient summary statistics much better and substantially cuts the simulation budget required for Phase VII's robustness sweeps.

**Pre-registered ABC-RF priors:**

| Parameter | Prior distribution | Range | Rationale |
|---|---|---|---|
| \(N_e\) | Log-uniform | \(10^4\)–\(10^5\) | *Rosa* population sizes |
| \(t_{hybrid}\) | Log-uniform | 500–5,000 generations | Pre-domestication timeframe |
| \(r\) (recombination) | From LDhat/ReLERNN | Estimated | Not constant |
| \(f_{clonal}\) | Uniform | 0.9–1.0 | Clonal propagation dominates |
| \(t_{domestication}\) | Log-uniform | 100–500 generations | Historical records |

**Summary statistics** will include:

- Ancestry-block length distribution (mean, variance, skewness);
- Topology weight distributions from Twisst;
- DFOIL and Δ-statistic values;
- \(F_{ST}\) between inferred ancestry blocks;
- **Site pattern frequency spectrum \(f_d\)** for comparison to DFOIL;
- SV enrichment at ancestry boundaries;
- Linkage disequilibrium decay curves within and between ancestry blocks.

**F5 threshold:** Bayes factor > 3 or ABC-RF posterior probability ratio > 0.66.

### 9.2 Directional introgression testing with DFOIL/Δ-statistics

The DFOIL method tests allelic patterns on the symmetric five-taxon tree \(S = (((1,2),(3,4)),5)\). More recent **Δ-statistics** generalize DFOIL and can incorporate or exclude singleton allelic patterns depending on taxon sampling, and can test asymmetric and quasisymmetric tree shapes.

**Explicit methodological decision:** DFOIL/Δ-statistics will be run **per-haplotype post-phasing** where phasing quality permits. If phasing quality is insufficient, they will be run on a **collapsed pseudo-diploid representation** using the two most divergent haplotypes at each locus.

**Pre-registered power analysis:** We will simulate tetraploid hybrid genomes with known directional introgression, phase them with realistic error, then apply DFOIL per-haplotype vs collapsed. We expect collapsed to lose power for recent introgression where introgressed haplotype frequency < 0.25. The results of this power analysis will be reported alongside empirical DFOIL results.

**Subgenome-specific testing:** With tetraploid *damascena*, we have 4 tips for the focal taxon. We will either (1) pick \(H_1\) as representative and test robustness across \(H_{1-4}\), or (2) explicitly test subgenome-specific introgression using Twisst on sub-genome-specific haplotype sets. Both will be done.

### 9.3 Topology weighting with Twisst

Twisst quantifies the contribution of each taxon topology to the full tree across genomic windows. Under ILS alone, the three possible topologies for a trio of taxa should appear in approximately equal frequencies. Under introgression, one topology will be overrepresented.

**Polyploid adaptation:** Twisst will be run on **sub-genome-specific haplotype sets** (e.g., the *R. gallica*-like sub-genome vs. the *R. moschata*-like sub-genome) to determine whether introgression signals are confined to one sub-genome. This requires high-quality phasing. As a phasing-free control, *twisst2* will be run on unphased genotype data of any ploidy.

### 9.4 Genome-wide multiple-testing correction

**Explicit correction is added to F1.** A window-by-window ancestry call will be considered "resolved" only if it passes a **false discovery rate (FDR) correction** (Benjamini-Hochberg) at the chromosome level, or a Bonferroni correction across all windows genome-wide. This threshold will be pre-registered alongside the existing falsification criteria.

