## 14. Human Selection and Clonal Propagation

The evolutionary history of *R. × damascena* cannot be separated from human history. Once a desirable phenotype became culturally and economically valuable, vegetative propagation could preserve particular genotypes.

$$
\text{evolutionary history} = \text{natural processes} + \text{hybridization} + \text{selection} + \text{propagation}
$$

The thesis will not interpret every present-day genomic feature as natural selection. A genomic feature may be ancestral, neutral, linked to a selected locus, introduced by introgression, preserved by clonal propagation, produced by structural mutation, or an artifact.

### 14.1 Biological hypothesis testing: continuous flowering

To strengthen the biological novelty, the thesis will test a specific biological hypothesis:

> **Hypothesis:** The *R. fedtschenkoana*-like ancestry is enriched in genes related to continuous flowering (a key trait of Damask roses).

This will be tested by comparing the ancestry state \(z_i\) at loci near known flowering genes (e.g., **CCD4**, **TFL1**) to the genome-wide background:

$$
P(z_i = F \mid \text{gene} \in \text{flowering}) > P(z_i = F \mid \text{genome-wide})
$$

**Permutation test:** Control for gene density by permuting gene locations while preserving local gene density, repeat content, and GC content. Report empirical p-values with FDR correction.

This links the genomic mosaic to a phenotype, which is a stronger biological contribution than ancestry inference alone.

