## 15. Proposed Research Workflow

### Phase 0 — Data and phasing audit (go/no-go gate)

**Pre-specified decision rules and audit table as described in Section 5.**

### Phase I — Literature and data audit

Build a complete evidence map of *R. × damascena* genomes, candidate ancestral genomes, plastomes, ITS sequences, population datasets, SV datasets, existing phylogenies, and published ancestry hypotheses.

### Phase II — Reproducibility and baseline

Reproduce the strongest published ancestry results using public data. Establish baseline D-statistics and ADMIXTURE results. If reproduction fails, that itself is an important finding.

### Phase III — Ancestry inference with model uncertainty

Implement the mixture-model HMM with inheritance-mode mixture and reference-bias correction. Generate local ancestry probabilities \(P(z_i \mid D)\) for each window and each candidate lineage. Run *twisst2*/*sticcs* on unphased data as a phasing-free control.

### Phase IV — Competing-model comparison

Use ABC-RF to formally compare Models A, B, C, and D. Apply DFOIL and Δ-statistics for directional introgression testing. Apply Twisst for topology weighting across the genome.

### Phase V — Mechanistic integration of structural variation

Overlay SVs onto ancestry blocks. Test for SV enrichment at ancestry boundaries with genomic background controls. Test for recombination suppression within inversions using LD decay analysis with recombination-rate confound control.

### Phase VI — Temporal and geographic inference

Estimate relative admixture times for different ancestry blocks using topology weights. Analyze phylogeographic structure of Moroccan accessions if population-level genomic data are available.

### Phase VII — Robustness and falsification

Repeat analyses under different reference genomes, candidate donor sets, window sizes, SNP filters, missing-data thresholds, and phylogenetic models. Apply quantitative falsification thresholds (Section 16).

